description.narrative:
Key results achieved during the reporting period, both planned and unplanned (positive, unforeseen outcomes) that have contributed to the overall success of the intervention:

Policy level:

- The National Risk Assessment (NRA) for Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing has been updated with an accompanying sectoral risk assessment of virtual assets (VAs) and proliferation financing in line with FATF recommendation 1;

- Guidelines for the integrity framework for top executive officials have been developed. The adoption of the Guidelines should regulate the conduct and appointment of top executive officials and contribute to the fulfilment of five GRECO recommendations, notably recommendations I, II, IV, VI and IX;

- Procedures for supervisory authorities in charge of beneficial ownership data verification have been developed.

Legislative level:

- The Law on Lobbying has been reviewed; 44 recommendations for improvement have been provided;

- The Law on Prevention of Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing and the Law on Games of Chance were amended in November 2024 to further align with international standards. Amendments include the key recommendations developed within the previous project.

Capacity building:

- The Financial Intelligence Unit - Administration for the Prevention of Money Laundering (APML) has received support to improve its efficiency by updating its prioritization plan for the selection of suspicious activity reports for analysis and further processing;

- Software for data collection and risk assessment in the real estate sector has been developed;

- Needs assessment for financial forensic experts has been prepared, which should result in tailor-made training programs and capacity building activities throughout the project. A capacity building program for supervisors in the games of chance sector has been developed and 15 supervisors of the Gaming Authority will accordingly be trained on updating supervisory tools in line with new the legal framework.

International Cooperation:

- International conference on civil forfeiture has been organized;

- Serbian representatives have been engaged in a regional workshop on emerging financial crime risks.

The results highlight significant progress in strengthening Serbia's AML/CFT regime and its alignment with international standards.

The key unexpected results include the following:

- Increased stakeholder engagement and higher than anticipated participation from various sectors in policy discussions and legislative reviews;

- Enhanced public awareness and understanding of economic crime issues due to media coverage and public outreach efforts.
